Test 1: Different Number of Neurons (exact clip duration, mean)
  10 neurons: 54%
  50 nuerons: 57%
  100 neurons: 63%
  200 neurons: 57%
  500 neurons: 46%

  More neurons doesnt always help and actually hurts performance, mainly due to dimensionality maybe with 384 trials we
  dont have enought data to train with 1000 feautres, the model starts to overfitt to noise. we need to get more data or use
  regularization or dimensionality reduction if we want to use more neurons. 

Test 2: Different time windows (100 neurons, mean activity, fixed windows)
  2 seconds: 44%
  5 seconds: 52%
  10 seconds: 57%

  longer windows give better performance, makes sense becuase more data to average over means less noise and more stable estimates
  longer windows also include more irrelevant activity after like the main neurons fired or smth. 

Test 3: Single Neuron vs Population (exact clip duration, mean activity)
  compared neuron 4 alone vs all 1455 neurons 
  neuron 4:  
    overall: 46%
    cinematic: 33%
    rendered 34%
    sports1m: 70%

  population:
    overall: 59%
    cinematic: 48%
    rendered: 73%
    sports1m: 59%

  neuron 4 is actually pretty good on its own especially for sports1m maybe this neuron likes something about sports its a sports fan
  but it doesnt do well with cinematic and rendered. population is more balanced along all three categories. Worse in sports tho. 

Test 4: Max vs Mean (100 neurons, exact clip duration)

  mean activity: 57%
  max activity: 45%

  mean is way better, max is more sensitive to outliers and noise. Could try other features like variance. 


Test 5: Fixed 5s window vs Exact clip duration (100 neurons, mean activity)

  fixed: 51%
  exact: 57%

  using exact clip duration is better, makes sense becuase clips are different lengths. 

Test 6: Neuron 4 fixed vs exact (single neuron, mean activity)

  fixed: 31%
  exact duration: 46%

  so perhaps exact time windows is the way to go ok good to know. Neuron 4 might have dynamics like how the neuron ramps up 
  that matter especially in sports since it did really well there. 


Conclusion: 

  Best Performance: 63% with 100 neurons, exact duration, mean activity. 
  More neurons help but only up to a point (dimensionality)
  longer time window helps but exact window seems better
  mean activity better than max
  population beats single neuron but not by much but then again only test neuron 4
  neuron 4 likes sports

  77 test trials is small 
  averaging over time gets rid of how the neuron ramps up to stimulus

  Can try: like a logistic regression to handle high dimensions which is something that struggled here
            k fold cross validation: split into 5 folds and rotate which is test set, better than standard 80/20
            try a nonlinear model maybe like svm.
